## Interface vs. Abstract Class in Java  
> **TL;DR:**  
> *An interface* is a contract that says “a class can do this.”  
> An *abstract class* is a partial implementation that “shares a common core” among subclasses.

Below is a comparison that covers the main differences you’ll see in everyday Java code (Java 17+).

| Feature | **Interface** | **Abstract Class** |
|---------|---------------|--------------------|
| **Inheritance** | `class C implements I1, I2, …` – a class can implement *any number* of interfaces. | `class D extends A` – a class can extend **only one** abstract class. |
| **Fields** | Only `public static final` constants (implicitly `public static final`). No instance fields. | Instance fields (private, protected, or public) are allowed. |
| **Constructors** | **No constructors** (cannot be instantiated). | Can declare constructors that subclasses invoke. |
| **Methods** |  
|   | • Pre‑Java 8: abstract methods only.  
|   | • Java 8+: `default` & `static` methods.  
|   | • Java 9+: private methods (even `static`). |  
|   | • Instance methods can be abstract or concrete. |  
| **Method visibility** | All abstract methods are implicitly `public`. Concrete `default`/`static` methods can be any visibility (usually `public`). | Can declare any visibility (`private`, `protected`, `public`) for instance methods. |
| **Multiple inheritance** | **Yes** – a class can implement several interfaces. | **No** – single inheritance only. |
| **Default implementation** | Starting with Java 8, interfaces can provide a *default* implementation that classes inherit, but they can still override it. | Abstract classes can provide concrete implementations that all subclasses inherit automatically. |
| **Static members** | `static` methods are allowed (Java 8+). They belong to the interface itself, not to any implementation. | Regular static methods/fields are allowed; they belong to the class. |
| **Extending vs. implementing** | `interface` is *implemented* (`implements`). | `abstract class` is *extended* (`extends`). |
| **Purpose (when to use)** | • Define a **capability** or role that can be mixed into any class.  
• Use when you need **multiple inheritance** or want to expose only a contract. | • Share common code or state (fields) among a set of closely related classes.  
• Use when you have a “is‑a” relationship and want to provide a shared implementation. |

---

## In‑Depth Look

### 1. Constructors & State
```java
abstract class Animal {
    protected String name; // state that all animals share

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

interface Named {
    // no constructor, no instance fields
}
```
*You can’t create an `Animal` instance directly; you can only create subclasses that call the constructor.*  
*You also can’t put instance fields in a `Named` interface.*

### 2. Multiple Implementation
```java
class FlyingFish extends Fish implements Flying {
    // implements two different roles
}
```
If `FlyingFish` tried to extend two abstract classes, it would be illegal.

### 3. Default Methods (Java 8+)
```java
interface Drivable {
    default void start() {
        System.out.println("Engine started");
    }
}
```
Any class that implements `Drivable` gets `start()` for free but can override it.

### 4. Private Methods in Interfaces (Java 9+)
```java
interface Logger {
    default void log(String msg) {
        if (shouldLog()) {
            System.out.println(msg);
        }
    }
    private boolean shouldLog() {
        return true; // helper not exposed
    }
}
```
These helpers reduce duplication inside default methods.

### 5. Static Methods in Interfaces
```java
interface Util {
    static int sum(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
}
```
Called as `Util.sum(1, 2);` – no instance needed.

### 6. Abstract Methods in Abstract Classes
```java
abstract class Vehicle {
    abstract void accelerate(); // must be overridden
}
```
Subclasses *must* provide an implementation.

---

## Practical Guidelines

| When to choose an **Interface** | When to choose an **Abstract Class** |
|---------------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| You want a **pure contract** – “any class that implements this can be used as…”. | You have a **family** of related classes that share code or fields. |
| You need **multiple inheritance** of behavior. | You need to control or share **state** or provide a **common constructor**. |
| The behavior is **additive** (you can mix it in). | The behavior is **inheritable** and might change the internal state. |
| You want to use **default methods** to provide optional implementations without forcing inheritance. | You want to enforce a “*is‑a*” relationship and maybe provide partial implementation. |
| You’re building a **public API** that may evolve; interfaces make it easier to add new methods later without breaking subclasses. | You’re building a **library** where the implementation details matter and you want to keep them hidden. |

---

## Quick Code Show‑and‑Tell

```java
// Interface example
public interface Serializable {
    // No fields; all methods are implicitly public
    void serialize();
}

// Abstract class example
public abstract class BaseEntity {
    private final UUID id = UUID.randomUUID();

    public UUID getId() { return id; }

    // Concrete method – shared by all entities
    public String toString() { return id.toString(); }

    // Abstract method – subclasses must implement
    public abstract String getName();
}
```

- `Serializable` can be mixed into any class: `class User implements Serializable`.
- `BaseEntity` can be extended by one subclass: `class User extends BaseEntity`.

---

## Bottom Line

| Interface | Abstract Class |
|-----------|----------------|
| *Pure contract* | *Partial implementation* |
| Multiple inheritance | Single inheritance |
| No instance fields or constructors | Instance fields & constructors |
| Methods are `public` (abstract) or `default`/`static` | Any visibility for methods |
| Best for “capabilities” or “roles” | Best for “shared code” among related classes |

Pick the one that matches the design intent:  
- **Interface** → *“I can do this”* (add-on).  
- **Abstract class** → *“I share common code”* (core).  

That’s the core difference. Happy coding!
